In article <2dngth$31i@pdq.coe.montana.edu> ericm@cs.montana.edu (Eric McWhorter) writes:
>Excellent observation.  Why can't monsters use things incorrectly
>at times, eg through extra healing or quarff paralization?  Also,
>if you find a corpse on the same square as an amulet, would help
>with identification.  The problem would be game balace.  You
>shouldn't be able to leave an AoS lying around and have a black
>dragon put it on, for example.  However, this would be amuzing
>at times rather than always annoying.

That's no real problem: just let amulets of strangulation disintegrate
after use... It would identify and at the same moment destroy the
objects. Monsters should only know the stuff they're generated with, and
things they recognize. Also monsters that are intelligent enough to read
scrolls of teleportation should be intelligent enough to read scrolls of
identify... Idiotic monsters would just wear and wield everything you
throw at them (The newt is flying high above you -more- He tries to take
his boots off but fails...)

I can imagine that it's difficult to code intelligent behaviour for
monsters, but you could make the rule that monsters will not wear things
that are usually generated cursed (there must be a table of
curse-chances somewhere in the code), and will generally distrust
_unidentified_ objects that may be bad things, like boots (could be cursed
levitation boots as well as speed boots) as opposed to shields (a
polished shield can of course be a cursed -5 shield of reflection - but
chances are small and - what the heck, it's still reflection ! ) This
way intelligent monsters would distrust all amulets, wands and potions,
and try them on somebody else first... just like you would. 

And if this all is to difficult to code, just let dumb creatures wear,
put on, quaff and zap on themselves all they find, and intelligent
creatures use only the things they know since creation day. 

